\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces The computation this thesis commits to. Segmentation proposes visible crates. Color type is a second decision on each mask. The count is not the number of masks: it is the number of occupied cells on a rectified lattice, times the depth of the pallet. The two shaded branches are the updates developed in Chapter~\ref {ch:methods}.}}{8}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Side view of the passage. The camera sees the front face only. Crates behind that face, indicated by the dashed extension, change the count and do not change the image. The bottom of the face sits just above the forks and the pallet; the top of the face may leave the frame when the stack is tall.}}{15}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Three of the four failures that define the problem. A merged seam shortens the tally by a whole crate. A lamp shift confuses the pairs whose reflectances are nearest. Hidden depth is invisible to any mask model that sees only the side face. The fourth failure, a shadowed or clipped cell, is the one the lattice gap rule is drawn to fill.}}{18}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Forward pass of the updated model. The backbone and the vertical gate are the baseline YOLO-vf-seg. The prototype assembly produces the mask. ICPH and PLC both read that mask and do not read each other. Depth enters PLC from outside the figure.}}{23}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces ICPH on one mask. The upper path is the chromatic feature that the estimate in Chapter~\ref {ch:system} actually trains. The lower path is the mask-pooled neck vector specified for the full segmenter. The linear layer is the prototype score. Both paths are cheap relative to the backbone.}}{24}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Chromaticity of the six design prototypes~\eqref {eq:prototypes}, plotted in the $(\chi _r,\chi _g)$ plane. Blue is omitted from the third coordinate and is small for amber and yellow, which is why those two classes approach each other when the illuminant estimate is noisy. White sits near the neutral point.}}{27}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces PLC on one frame. Rectification, origin, and snapping use the known pitch. Gap completion is a single morphological step on the integer grid. Depth is an external reading. No step is learned.}}{37}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Detection probability~\eqref {eq:detprob} against blur, for a stack tall enough to have a distinct bottom, middle, and top. The gap rule is asked to repair the bottom curve, not the middle one.}}{46}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces The stepped stack of the worked example. The top row truly contains one crate. Gap completion fills the cell beside it and does not fill the corner, because the corner is visited first and has only one orthogonal neighbor at that moment.}}{47}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces How a booked count is assembled, in the letters of~\eqref {eq:front-signed}. The detector contributes misses and false cells. The gap rule puts some misses back and may add phantoms. Depth scales the front count and, when the reading is wrong, adds or removes a whole rank. The dashed arrow is the comparison the estimate computes. It is not an input to the system.}}{50}{figure.caption.17}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Data flow of one passage. The camera's white balance is locked. The buffer holds a handful of frames. The segmenter runs on each. ICPH scores every mask. PLC runs once, on the frame the margins select. The depth reading joins only at the multiplication.}}{53}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The same numbers as \Cref {tab:color}. Classical correction sits near chance for every severity. Raw matching wins only while the lamp is nearly white. ICPH degrades more slowly once the severity leaves that regime.}}{58}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Mean absolute error of the six counters, from \Cref {tab:count}. The vertical axis is crates per passage, on a mean load of about $23$ crates. PLC on one frame is the lowest bar. The five-frame union is not.}}{60}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Plan-and-elevation sketch of the gate, not to a common scale. The camera is fixed, the face plane is the calibration plane of $H$, and the standoff is the $Z$ used in~\eqref {eq:fov}. The truck path is perpendicular to the optical axis at the moment PLC is meant to run.}}{64}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces The passage the specification actually runs, collected onto one page so the estimate and the build can be read together. Frames are not unioned. Depth joins at PLC. The weekly calibration moves only the abstention threshold. The shift sum is where a positive bias of under one crate per pallet becomes a reconciliation against production, and where the much larger negative bias of a mask tally would appear if the lattice were removed.}}{71}{figure.caption.28}%
